<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Septimania</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Septimania was the coastal region of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narbonne">Narbonne</a>, roughly modern Languedoc-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roussillon">Roussillon</a>. It was the only part of Gaul the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> kept after Vouillé in 507, was taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> around 720, and was conquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-the-short">Pepin the Short</a>, who captured Narbonne in 759; it then became a Carolingian march.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/septimania.jpg" alt="Septimania" width="320" height="229" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: File:537Septimania3.JPG: The original uploader was Polylerus at English Wikipedia. / *derivative work Niczar · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Septimania_in_537_AD.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
